aps to a circle \u03c9_2 passing through D and T' (image of T on O). Their second intersection X' is the image of X.\n\n3. Observe that B' and Q' lie on \u2113, so chord B'Q' of \u03c9_1 lies on \u2113. Hence \u03c9_1 is symmetric with respect to \u2113? Not needed.\n\n4. Note that T' is the second intersection of line DT with O, i.e., the inverse of T. Since T lies on \u2113, T' is the pole of \u2113 with respect to O? Actually the pole of \u2113 is D, not T'. So not.\n\n5. Claim: The circles \u03c9_1 and \u03c9_2 intersect again at a point on \u2113. Reason: Both circles pass through D, and their other intersection is the pole of line BQ with respect to the polar pair (D, \u2113). But the pole of line BQ is the intersection of the tangents at B and Q, which we denote M. Under inversion, M maps to a point M' on \u2113. Moreover, M' is precisely the second intersection of \u03c9_1 and \u03c9_2. Therefore M' lies on \u2113, establishing collinearity.\n\nNeed to justify that M' is indeed the second intersection.\n\nAlternatively, we can argue using the fact that inversion preserves harmonic pencils. The pencil of lines through D includes lines DB, DQ, DT, etc. Under inversion, this pencil maps to a pencil of circles through D. The line BQ corresponds to a circle through D, B', Q'; line DT corresponds to a circle through D, T', ?. The intersection of these two circles (other than D) corresponds to the harmonic conjugate of D with respect to the pencil? Not.\n\nMaybe we can use the concept of \"inverse of a line\" being a circle through the center, and the intersection of inverses corresponds to the pole of the original line.\n\nSpecifically, given two lines \u21131 and \u21132 not through D, their inverses are circles \u03c91, \u03c92 through D. Their second intersection (besides D) is the inverse of the pole of the pair of lines \u21131, \u21132? Something like that.\n\nRecall theorem: For a fixed point D (center of inversion), the inverse of a line \u2113 not passing